# Design: add-codex-skill-update

## Context

The Scheme C archive chose reproducible, reviewed dependency updates over silent
latest-version tracking. Its README maintenance procedure already distinguishes
source/runtime copies, Git+symlink checkouts, marketplace plugins, and installer
snapshots. What is missing is the executable control plane that turns those
rules into regular detection, a reviewable plan, an approval boundary, and
transactional evidence.

The current environment demonstrates why a generic updater is unsafe:

- Superpowers is exposed through a symlink to a Git checkout;
- the checkout has a local commit and is also behind upstream;
- upstream and installation-channel versions may differ;
- marketplace refresh is not equivalent to installed-plugin replacement;
- copied Skills retain no automatic connection to their original ref.

The accepted dependency-maintenance direction is named **Update Policy U1** in
this contract: automate freshness detection, never automate update approval.
U1 is subordinate to the retained workflow Scheme C and is unrelated to the
earlier workflow Scheme A that would replace Superpowers.

## Goals / Non-Goals

### Goals

- Give the user one discoverable, explicit maintenance Skill.
- Detect stale, diverged, lagging, unmanaged, unknown, and Major-blocked states
  without changing managed targets.
- Make every version claim traceable to source, timestamp, ref/hash, and
  installation mode.
- Bind an update to an immutable current-state/candidate plan and an exact user
  decision.
- Validate in isolation, promote atomically where safe, prove fresh discovery,
  and preserve a verified rollback path.
- Keep scheduled execution read-only with respect to every managed Skill.
- Preserve Scheme C, Router ownership, OpenSpec Major gates, and Git/publication
  authority.

### Non-Goals

- A general package manager for arbitrary files or binaries.
- A native extension to the `codex` CLI command parser.
- Automatic conflict resolution or automatic rebasing of local Skill patches.
- Automatic acceptance of release notes, semantic version labels, or upstream
  trust.
- Cross-CLI installation of this optional Codex maintenance Skill.
- A resident daemon, remote service, credential store, or telemetry service.
- Updating an unregistered Skill by guessing its source.

## Decisions

### 1. Independent explicit Skill, Router-governed mutation

`codex-skill-update` is a sibling source and runtime Skill with a narrowly scoped
`allow_implicit_invocation: true` policy so the user-requested natural-language
audit phrases are discoverable. Its public Skill and CLI provide only
non-mutating audit, plan, verify, and report inspection. A mutation request
immediately returns to the Router and cannot call an update transaction from the
public Skill path. `$codex-skill-update` remains the deterministic explicit
invocation form.

Read-only `audit`, `plan`, and `verify` run directly through the controller.
Apply, user-requested post-success rollback, schedule installation, schedule
removal, and rollback-material cleanup re-enter
`openspec-superpower-change` because they change global runtime state. A Major
version or any trigger, routing, authority, evidence, completion, or
installation-lifecycle change requires an approved OpenSpec contract in
addition to the exact action approval. Automatic compensation after an approved
apply failure is part of that apply transaction and needs no second approval.

The Router source, trigger, portable manifest, and cross-CLI runtime copies do
not change merely to install the optional updater.

### 2. User interface does not impersonate Codex CLI

The user-facing forms are:

| Intent | Conversational form | Script form |
|---|---|---|
| Audit group | `Codex Skill 更新巡检` | `codex_skill_update.py audit --group workflow-core` |
| Audit one | `检查 Superpowers 更新` | `codex_skill_update.py audit --skill superpowers` |
| Plan | `生成 Superpowers 升级计划` | `codex_skill_update.py plan --skill superpowers` |
| Apply | `批准应用升级计划 <plan-id>` | Router-only internal transaction; no public mutation CLI |
| Verify | `验证升级回执 <receipt-id>` | `codex_skill_update.py verify --receipt <receipt-path>` |
| Roll back | `回滚 Skill 更新 <receipt-id>` | Router-only internal transaction; no public mutation CLI |
| Plan registry replacement | `生成 Skill registry 替换计划` | Router-only internal non-mutating planner |
| Replace registry | `批准替换 Skill registry <plan-id>` | Router-only internal transaction |
| Plan schedule | `生成每周只读巡检变更计划` | `codex_skill_update.py plan --schedule {install,remove,replace}` |
| Install schedule | `安装每周只读巡检 <schedule-plan-id>` | Router-only internal transaction |
| Replace schedule | `替换每周只读巡检 <schedule-plan-id>` | Router-only internal transaction |
| Remove schedule | `移除每周只读巡检 <schedule-plan-id>` | Router-only internal transaction |

An optional shell wrapper may be named `codex-skill-update`; the implementation
must not shadow `codex`, patch shell completion to invent a native subcommand, or
document `codex skill update` as an existing CLI capability. The public script
parser exposes only non-mutating `audit`, `plan`, `verify`, and report/metadata
inspection. Mutation entrypoints are internal library/adapter calls used only
after the Router records a bound approval artifact.

### 3. Registry and four-value version ledger

Controller state defaults to `${CODEX_HOME}/skill-update`. A
`CODEX_SKILL_UPDATE_HOME` override is allowed for isolated tests or an explicitly
configured runtime only after validation. Before its first write, the controller
resolves every existing path component without following an untrusted final
symlink, verifies current-user ownership and `0700` directory/`0600` file
permissions, and rejects an unsafe state root. After registry load it constructs
a global managed-root ownership graph across every package's source, runtime,
release, discovery, projection, candidate, backup, and managed target plus the
controller state root and Bootstrap Control Root. It compares normalized lexical containment, `lstat`,
real paths, and device/inode identity for existing objects. The controller
state root and OS temporary root, plus a manager-owned common discovery
container or release store, are non-package graph anchors. A reviewed fixed
layout may contain distinct exact children/subtrees leased to controller roles,
transactions, or packages, but no package may claim or mutate the whole
container; ownership and overlap checks operate on those exact leases.
Controller-state metadata/backup/journal children remain one declared
controller domain rather than fabricated package roots.
Except for the two exact declared projections, independent package-owned nodes
may not overlap, contain, or alias one another. The graph is rebuilt before
audit, plan, transaction recovery, apply, rollback, schedule mutation, and
cleanup. The JSON registry is runtime-specific and is never copied into a
public repository.

The registry separates an updateable **package** from its discovered **Skill
entries**. A package is one source/install/version/update unit; each Skill entry
maps a discovered name and path back to exactly one package. One package may
expose multiple entries. Superpowers is therefore one Git package and one
collection symlink with many `superpowers:*` entries, never one independent
update target per discovered sub-Skill.

Each registered package contains:

- canonical name, package kind, user/account/project scope, dependency group,
  required/optional state, and consumer;
- authoritative targets and projection targets such as collection symlinks;
- installation mode and resolved source/runtime roots;
- upstream locator and pinned/default ref;
- tested compatibility baseline;
- effective-local and installation-channel observation adapter IDs from a
  closed built-in enum; registry values supply data only;
- candidate validation commands as argv arrays and contained working roots;
- local patch policy, risk policy, and rollback adapter;
- README/receipt obligations where applicable.

Each discovered Skill entry contains its public name, package ID, relative
Skill path, discovery root, required/optional state, and expected metadata hash.
Duplicate names from different packages or an entry that escapes its package
root are `MULTIPLE_SOURCES` plus `BLOCKED`.

`manual-local`, Codex system, and manager-owned plugin packages are audit-only
until their authoritative manager/source and reversible adapter are proven.
System directories and plugin caches are never generic replacement targets.

Every audit records four independent values:

1. `tested_compatibility_baseline`;
2. `effective_local_version`;
3. `latest_observed_upstream`;
4. `installation_channel_available`.

Each value carries evidence timestamp and source. Missing, stale, incomparable,
or conflicting evidence is explicit and cannot collapse to `CURRENT`.

The initial `workflow-core` package group contains Router, Companion,
Superpowers, and the updater. Discovery may report additional `UNMANAGED` Skill
entries, but only an explicit package plus entry mapping expands the managed
guarantee.

The initial bootstrap plan binds the canonical registry bytes, schema/revision
and hash, complete package/entry/projection closure, selected adapter identities
and schemas, and exact controller-state layout. Bootstrap may install only that
bound registry. Before planning or destination backup, the external Router
loads those candidate bytes as data and builds the complete ownership graph
against every observed pre-state target plus the Bootstrap Control Root; it does
not require or create a destination registry to perform that check. The
controller never edits the installed registry implicitly. A later change uses a
fresh canonical `registry-replace` plan and exact Router action approval,
journal, conditional final-child mutation, verification, and receipt. Any
registry hash or closure change invalidates the installed schedule-execution
binding and requires a separately approved `schedule-replace` before the job
may resume package/network/notification activity.

### 4. Read-only audit and one-shot scheduling

`audit` is read-only with respect to managed Skills and installation channels.
Observation and notification select only reviewed built-in adapter IDs. Each
adapter has a fixed absolute executable and argv schema, rejects unknown flags,
uses no shell or code interpolation, and treats registry values, refs, package
names, and notification text as data. Before invocation, external executables
pass no-follow owner/type/mode checks and match the adapter's reviewed content
hash or platform code identity. Each adapter also owns a scrubbed fixed
environment and disables user/system config, hooks, credential/proxy/remote
helpers, interactive prompts, and mutation-capable protocols unless that exact
helper/protocol is separately reviewed and bound. Unsupported/private
observation returns `UNKNOWN/BLOCKED` instead of executing ambient config. The
registry cannot supply executable paths or raw commands. Audit may:

- inspect filesystem metadata, real paths, symlinks, manifests, Git status and
  refs;
- use network read operations such as remote-ref lookup;
- invoke only the fixed read-only installed-version or remote-ref probes owned
  by those adapters;
- write sanitized reports and its own lock/timestamp under the controller state
  root.

It may not fetch into a live checkout, refresh a marketplace, install/remove a
plugin, replace a snapshot, edit a registry automatically, or call `apply`.
Channel-state snapshots around every external probe are verification evidence
that a read-only adapter did not mutate the checkout, marketplace, plugin set,
or installer snapshot.

For the current macOS runtime, a Router-governed schedule transaction renders a
user LaunchAgent that runs one non-interactive audit every Monday at 10:00 local
time. The job exits after producing its report; it is not a resident daemon.
Apply/rollback commands are absent from the template. The controller treats a
missing or older-than-eight-days successful report as stale and refreshes audit
evidence on demand.

Schedule installation/removal is separate from execution of the scheduled
audit. The public `plan --schedule` path may create a non-mutating canonical
schedule plan, but installation, removal, and replacement remain Router-only
transactions. Every plan binds:

- action `schedule-install`, `schedule-remove`, or `schedule-replace`;
- label `com.openai.codex.skill-update-audit` and launch domain
  `gui/<current-uid>`;
- exact plist path
  `<current-user-home>/Library/LaunchAgents/com.openai.codex.skill-update-audit.plist`
  and allowed-path set;
- exact program argv whose controller script path is inside one
  content-addressed immutable payload rather than the movable discovery
  symlink; interpreter/executable identity, payload digest, runtime-lock hash,
  exact payload closure, and expected active updater discovery-entry
  `lstat`/`readlink`/resolved-target identity;
- canonical controller state-root identity, registry schema/revision/hash,
  dependency-group ID, complete package/entry/projection closure, and every
  selected observation/notification adapter ID plus executable/code identity
  and argv-schema hash;
- `Weekday=1`, `Hour=10`, and `Minute=0` (launchd uses `0` and `7` for
  Sunday);
- observed pre-state: absent/present plist hash and mode plus a normalized
  effective loaded-configuration fingerprint;
- candidate plist canonical hash for install/replace;
- creation/expiry timestamps and exact automatic-failure rollback strategy.

The normalized loaded fingerprint covers the label, domain, program, argv,
calendar, and other controller-owned effective fields used for restore and
verification. A loaded label whose effective configuration cannot be proven to
come from the exact observed plist, including a loaded label with no plist, is
`BLOCKED_SCHEDULE_DRIFT`; the controller does not guess how to restore it.
Changing an existing registration is `schedule-replace`, never an implicit
install. After the exact schedule-plan decision, the Router records a matching
action-bound approval artifact, resolves the exact plist and LaunchAgent label,
backs up any pre-existing registration outside discovery roots, mutates an
expected-absent plist only with atomic no-replace or an expected-present plist
only with the conditional exchange protocol in Section 8, verifies `launchctl`
state and rendered arguments, and
automatically restores the prior registration on failure. Its canonical
schedule receipt binds plan and approval hashes, before/after plist hashes and
modes, before/after effective loaded fingerprints, schedule-execution binding,
validation evidence, timestamp, and rollback availability. A post-success
removal or replacement requires a fresh current plan and approval; a receipt
alone grants no mutation.

The installed job receives the approved schedule-execution binding and rechecks
its own exact payload path/digest, canonical runtime lock and exact closure,
expected active discovery-entry identity, interpreter/executable identity, the
state-root identity, registry hash, group ID, adapter identities, and complete
closure before reading any package, contacting any upstream, or emitting a
notification. A controller runtime/discovery or registry change therefore
requires a new schedule-replace plan and approval; the old job cannot follow a
movable symlink into new code.
Drift writes no ordinary audit result, returns
`BLOCKED_SCHEDULE_BINDING_DRIFT`, and requires a new schedule-replace plan and
approval. Tests snapshot managed targets and installation channels before and
after the already-installed audit job runs; only the bound controller state root
may change during a successfully bound execution.

When audit finds an update, blocker, unmanaged/unknown required package, or
audit failure, the job emits one local macOS notification containing only
package names and headline statuses. `CURRENT` produces no notification.
Notification text contains no paths, refs, hashes, commands, or error details;
delivery success/failure is recorded in the sanitized report and cannot convert
a failed audit into success.

Other platforms retain the portable one-shot command and documented scheduling
contract; their OS scheduler installation is outside this change.

### 5. Deterministic freshness, observations, eligibility, and result codes

Audit keeps four orthogonal fields instead of forcing simultaneous facts into
one ambiguous status:

| Field | Values |
|---|---|
| `freshness_status` | `CURRENT`, `UPDATE_AVAILABLE`, `UNMANAGED`, `UNKNOWN` |
| `observations` | zero or more of `DIVERGED`, `CHANNEL_LAG`, `STALE_EVIDENCE`, `MULTIPLE_SOURCES` |
| `apply_eligibility` | `ELIGIBLE`, `BLOCKED`, `BLOCKED_MAJOR` |
| `reason_codes` | sorted unique machine-readable blocker/detail codes |

`freshness_status` uses the deterministic precedence
`UNMANAGED > UNKNOWN > UPDATE_AVAILABLE > CURRENT`.
`apply_eligibility` uses `BLOCKED_MAJOR > BLOCKED > ELIGIBLE`.
Observations never disappear merely because a stronger apply blocker exists.
`BLOCKED_MAJOR` reason codes map to `apply_eligibility=BLOCKED_MAJOR`; specialized
codes including `BLOCKED_LAYOUT_MIGRATION`, `BLOCKED_RELEASE_COLLISION`,
`BLOCKED_SELF_UPDATE`, `BLOCKED_CHANNEL_MUTATION`,
`BLOCKED_SCHEDULE_DRIFT`, `BLOCKED_SCHEDULE_BINDING_DRIFT`,
`BLOCKED_ROOT_ALIAS`, and `BLOCKED_RECOVERY_REQUIRED` map to
`apply_eligibility=BLOCKED`. All reason codes remain visible; they do not
compete in the headline precedence.
For compact display only, the headline precedence is
`BLOCKED_MAJOR > BLOCKED > UNMANAGED > UNKNOWN > DIVERGED >
UPDATE_AVAILABLE > CHANNEL_LAG > CURRENT`; the complete structured fields and
evidence remain authoritative.

Mutation attempts use a separate single-valued `transaction_result`:
`SUCCEEDED`, `BLOCKED`, `FAILED_COMPENSATED`, or `RECOVERY_REQUIRED`.
`FAILED_COMPENSATED` means failure plus verified restoration;
`RECOVERY_REQUIRED` means no success/restoration claim is allowed and all new
work stops. A table-driven fixture covers field mapping, reason retention, and
every valid transaction-result transition.

The known Superpowers checkout therefore reports `DIVERGED` in observations and
`BLOCKED_MAJOR` for apply eligibility when the candidate crosses the approved
Major boundary. A table-driven compound fixture covers every precedence pair.

Candidate inspection also produces a capability-surface diff. New or changed
scripts, executable modes, hooks, MCP servers, apps, tools, implicit-invocation
policy, trigger scope, or dependency closure cannot be hidden inside a semantic
version label; they force `BLOCKED_MAJOR` and an updated reviewed contract.

### 6. Immutable plan and approval binding

`plan` stages or retrieves a fixed candidate only in a restrictive
`mktemp`-created OS temporary directory outside the state, discovery, source,
runtime, release, and managed target roots. A normal plan is written under the
validated controller state root. The first-install `bootstrap-apply` plan is
instead written under the current immutable authorization workspace in the
restrictive Router-owned Bootstrap Control Root
`${CODEX_HOME}/openspec-superpower-change/bootstrap-control/codex-skill-update/`,
which the exact Major source-implementation approval creates outside every
updater destination before bootstrap planning. That root uses verified
no-follow current-user `0700` directories/`0600` files and is itself included
in the global ownership graph. No bootstrap authority record, plan, approval,
lease, journal, receipt, or closeout evidence is written to the
not-yet-approved updater state root.
Any missing fixed ancestor between the already validated `${CODEX_HOME}` and
the package root is created one component at a time with directory-FD-anchored
no-replace semantics, restrictive mode, parent fsync, and post-create
owner/type/mode/device/inode verification. The shared
`openspec-superpower-change/` and `bootstrap-control/` directories are
manager-owned graph anchors, not package roots; existing exact safe anchors may
retain only their separately declared child leases.

The final package root is built as a restrictive same-filesystem sibling
candidate whose fixed closure initially contains canonical
`control-root.json`, a verified mode-`0600` single-link `operation.lock`, and
mode-`0700` `authorizations/`. The stable marker binds only schema, absolute
target path, package ID, and Router control-plane ID; it does not bind one
decision, session instance, change-id, or Major manifest. The candidate name is
bound to the stable marker hash and is a temporary graph lease. Atomic
no-replace promotion plus parent fsync serializes root creation. A matching
stranded candidate or target with that exact stable marker and closure is
resume/reuse-only. An unmarked empty target, mismatched marker, unsafe ancestor,
undeclared sibling, or foreign/ambiguous content blocks without repair,
overwrite, deletion, or guessed adoption.

This fixed-root initializer is the sole exception to the rule that an
entrypoint acquires the controller operation lock first. Before promotion it
may only verify/create the disclosed parent anchors and candidate/root closure;
it may not probe updater targets, create a plan, use network/package adapters,
or write any updater destination. Immediately after promotion/reuse it opens,
validates, and acquires the embedded operation lock before any other work.

Under that lock, each Major manifest gets one immutable mode-`0700`
`authorizations/<manifest-sha256>/` workspace. Its canonical marker binds the
manifest hash, change-id, raw artifact hashes, projection digest, decision
provenance/time, and control-plane instance. Its fixed child layout contains
only verified no-overwrite content-addressed copies of the Major manifest/raw
snapshots, short authority lock and append-only revocations, plans, approvals,
leases, journals, receipts, and closeout evidence. Creation is same-filesystem
no-replace plus fsync; an existing
workspace is reuse-only after exact marker/closure verification. Reapproval
creates a new manifest-hash workspace, preserves every old workspace and
receipt, and never edits the stable root marker. A known journal in the selected
workspace enters recovery; a mismatched or ambiguous workspace blocks. Before
selecting or creating any workspace, the locked Router enumerates and validates
the complete declared workspace set. Any incomplete journal in an older
workspace must finish lease-bound recovery or block all new work; reapproval
cannot bypass it.

The controller derives `plan-id` from canonical plan bytes and removes the
temporary candidate after recording its immutable provenance and fingerprint.
Apply retrieves a fresh isolated candidate and requires the same fingerprint.
Each adapter defines a canonical complete candidate fingerprint:
a sorted `lstat` inventory of path, type, mode, regular-file content hash,
symlink target, and link multiplicity for filesystem material, rejecting
escapes and undeclared hardlinks; and, for Git, repository/object format,
commit/tree/ref, submodule identities, plus the same exact checked-out closure.
A ref name or semantic version alone is insufficient.

The plan binds:

- registry schema and registry revision/hash;
- canonical package ID, complete expected discovered-entry/projection set, and
  resolved authoritative and projection targets;
- current source/runtime fingerprint and symlink metadata;
- candidate source ref, immutable fingerprint, and provenance;
- installation adapter and exact allowed mutation paths;
- validation executable/code identity, argv/cwd, scrubbed environment,
  network/write-containment policy, fixed adapter/boundary timeouts, and
  expected evidence;
- risk classification, OpenSpec requirement, stop conditions, and rollback
  adapter;
- for Major eligibility, a `major_authorization` binding with exact change-id,
  immutable authorization-manifest SHA-256, approved raw
  proposal/design/spec/tasks SHA-256 set, canonical contract-projection digest,
  direct-user decision provenance, and decision timestamp;
- creation/expiry timestamps.

Creating a plan grants no approval. Before apply, the controller recomputes all
bindings and rejects stale/tampered plans. After the user's exact decision, the
Router records a canonical approval artifact that binds:

- schema version, decision owner, and control-plane instance;
- decision provenance `direct-user-confirmation`;
- action (`apply`, `bootstrap-apply`, `rollback`, `registry-replace`,
  `schedule-install`, `schedule-remove`, `schedule-replace`, or `cleanup`);
- target, plan/receipt ID, allowed-path-set hash, and scope hash;
- the identical Major authorization binding when the plan is Major;
- creation and expiry timestamps.

The internal transaction entrypoint accepts only a current canonical approval
artifact whose hash and fields match the requested action. The artifact is
auditable decision evidence, not cryptographic human authentication; the Router
remains responsible for observing and accurately recording the user decision.
The public CLI cannot mint an approval or invoke a mutation. Platform permission
alone is insufficient. Missing, stale, or hash-mismatched Major evidence remains
`BLOCKED_MAJOR` even when every other plan field matches.

A later direct user revocation is recorded through an independent Router-owned
authority channel: `authority/` under the validated normal state root, or under
the selected Bootstrap authorization workspace. The channel has one
current-user-owned mode-`0600`, single-link, no-follow `authority.lock` and an
append-only `revocations/<approval-sha256>.json` namespace. A revocation is
canonical sorted-key compact UTF-8 JSON containing exactly schema `1`, kind
`approval-revocation`, approval SHA-256, decision provenance/owner/control-plane
instance, and UTC RFC 3339 decision time. The Router creates it no-replace,
fsyncs the file and parents, and reuses an existing path only after exact
regular-file canonical-content verification. Public paths cannot mint it;
unsafe links, modes, owners, closure, or collisions block authority use.

The short authority lock never requires or acquires the transaction-long
operation lock, so the Router can record revocation while an operation is
running. A forward transaction already holding the operation lock acquires the
authority lock in that fixed nesting order, validates the complete authority
closure, and holds the short lock from its expiry/revocation check through the
durable boundary intent, atomic target mutation, all required fsyncs, and the
durable boundary result. The journal records the UTC/monotonic authority-check
time; expiry at or before that instant blocks, while a later expiry is ordered
after this one bounded boundary and blocks the next. It then releases the
authority lock before validation or other long non-mutating work. This is the
linearization point: a revocation
ordered first blocks the boundary; one ordered after a started boundary blocks
the next forward boundary and activates only lease-bound restoration once
intent exists. Terminal success receipt/lease consumption is itself such a
boundary; a revocation ordered after it is non-retroactive and requires a new
rollback decision rather than guessed rollback.

Before any checklist marker is changed after approval, the controller stores
the exact approved bytes of each of the four contract artifacts at
`openspec/changes/add-codex-skill-update/approvals/artifacts/<artifact-sha256>`
using verified-parent, no-follow, no-overwrite creation. An existing snapshot
is reuse-only after an exact regular-file/content match; a collision or unsafe
ancestor blocks progress. These snapshots make the approved raw hash set
reproducible after checklist-only progress changes.

Each authorization manifest is written once from its user-approved revision at
`openspec/changes/add-codex-skill-update/approvals/<manifest-sha256>.json`.
It is canonical UTF-8 JSON with sorted keys and compact separators using exactly
these fields: `schema` with integer `1`; `change_id`;
`artifact_hashes` as a path-to-SHA-256 object for the four contract artifacts;
`contract_projection_sha256`; `source_bootstrap_prestate_sha256`;
`source_bootstrap_material_hashes` as the exact path-to-SHA-256 object for the
complete approved untracked source/supporting inventory; `source_bootstrap_git` containing
exactly `executable_path`, `executable_sha256`, `version`, `repository_root`,
`git_common_dir`, `object_format`, `base_commit`, `base_tree`, `branch_ref`,
`worktree_path`, and `worktree_admin_path`; `source_bootstrap_sandbox`
containing exactly `executable_path`, `executable_sha256`, `profile_sha256`,
`write_allowlist_sha256`, `network_policy` (`DENY`),
`child_exec_policy` (`HELPER_AND_GIT_BINARY_ONLY`), and
`source_bootstrap_helper` containing exactly `executable_path`,
`executable_sha256`, `fixed_argv`, `fixed_environment`, and
`fd_binding_protocol` (which SHALL list every retained mutable-parent FD
required before `exec`); `decision_provenance` with value
`direct-user-confirmation`; `decision_timestamp` as a UTC RFC 3339 string; and
`control_plane_instance`. It contains no transcript or private prompt. Its own
SHA-256 is both the manifest identity and filename. Reapproval creates new
content-addressed snapshots as needed plus a new immutable manifest and never
rewrites prior evidence.

From the user decision through raw snapshots, manifest/profile/journal setup,
and successful task 2.1 post-verification, all approved material paths are
byte-frozen. No checklist marker may change and tasks 1.5/2.1 remain visually
unchecked during that interval. Task 2.1 initially materializes every exact
approved byte stream once, no-overwrite. Only after that exact copy and
post-verification may task 1.5/2.1 markers and later checklist progress change
under the normalized projection rule. Any pre-bootstrap marker or other byte
drift blocks and requires a newly approved revision; it is never reconciled by
overwriting the worktree copy.

The contract projection replaces only a line-leading Markdown marker matching
`- [ ]`, `- [x]`, or `- [X]` in `proposal.md` and `tasks.md` with `- [ ]`;
`design.md` and the spec bytes are unchanged. It hashes a domain-prefixed,
sorted-key compact JSON object
`{"files":[{"path":...,"sha256":...}],"schema":1}` with files sorted by
UTF-8 bytewise change-root-relative path in this exact order: `design.md`,
`proposal.md`, `specs/skill-update-governance/spec.md`, and `tasks.md`. The
exact UTF-8 prefix is
`openspec-major-contract-projection-v1` followed by one NUL byte. The projection
does not drop, reorder, or normalize any other bytes. Only after task 2.1 exact
materialization, later `[ ]`/`[x]` progress changes do not rewrite the manifest.
Before that boundary every byte is frozen. Any other
proposal/design/spec/tasks byte change alters the projection digest, invalidates
Preflight/plan eligibility, and requires a new exact approval and manifest.

### 7. Adapter capability is explicit

All four installation modes support audit and a canonical planning result. A
mode that cannot obtain and fingerprint an isolated candidate still returns its
exact blocker and resume condition, but it does not emit an actionable
`plan-id`; a diagnostic planning result cannot be approved or passed to apply.
Only an actionable update plan satisfying Section 6 may receive a later Router
approval. Apply is allowed only when the selected adapter proves:

- exact real target and contained allowed paths;
- isolated candidate;
- target-specific validation;
- atomic promotion on the current filesystem;
- deterministic fresh discovery/version verification;
- verified rollback.

`source-runtime` and `installer-snapshot` apply only to an already managed
versioned layout: immutable release directories outside the discovery entry,
plus one discovery symlink switched with the same-filesystem conditional
final-child protocol defined in Section 8.
The controller never claims to atomically replace an existing non-empty
directory. An existing plain copied runtime is audit/plan-only and reports
`BLOCKED_LAYOUT_MIGRATION`; converting it to the managed layout is a separate
approved lifecycle migration.

`git-symlink` similarly atomically retargets a symlink to a separately validated
immutable checkout; it never rebases or overwrites an existing divergent
checkout. A user-requested plan may create only a fixed ephemeral candidate
object/checkout store under its OS temporary root and removes it after
fingerprinting. An exact apply approval may create only the bound immutable
checkout under the allowed release root. Neither action initializes or changes
a sibling source, live checkout, or other user repository, and neither grants
staging, commit, branch/tag/remote mutation, reset, clean, push, or publication.
Both use a closed Git materialization adapter with fixed executable/code
identity, scrubbed environment, disabled ambient config/hooks/helpers/prompts,
and bound protocol/ref policy.
Fault-injection tests cover interruption before release finalization, before
symlink replacement, immediately after replacement, and during post-promotion
verification. Version 1 keeps Codex plugin replacement
`BLOCKED_CHANNEL_MUTATION`; remove/reinstall is not an implicit fallback. A
future manager mutation requires a new approved contract and action model.

#### Runtime payload integrity

`references/runtime-manifest.json` is a repository-only build allowlist and
schema declaring exact relative paths and expected normalized modes, not a file
copied into the discovered payload and not a self-referential hash list. The
builder resolves it into a canonical `runtime-lock.json` with:

- schema version `1`;
- sorted POSIX relative paths with no absolute path, empty segment, `.`, `..`,
  backslash, NUL, or traversal representation;
- regular-file type only, normalized mode `0644` or `0755`, and SHA-256 content
  digest for every entry; hard-linked files (`st_nlink != 1`) are rejected;
- only reviewed runtime top-level paths and no repository-only or sensitive
  category.

Canonical lock bytes are UTF-8 JSON with sorted keys and compact separators.
The payload identifier is SHA-256 over a fixed schema/domain prefix plus those
canonical bytes. A release is
`<release-store>/<payload-sha256>/payload/` plus sibling
`runtime-lock.json`; the discovery symlink targets the exact `payload/`
directory. The stored lock must itself be canonical and recompute to the
release-directory digest. Payload closure is exact: every lock entry must exist
with matching type, mode, and digest, every directory must be mode `0755`, and
no extra entry or symlink is permitted.

The digest directory has exact top-level closure: it is a current-user-owned
mode-`0755` directory containing only `payload/` and `runtime-lock.json`.
No-follow checks reject a symlink in any existing release-path component. The
lock is a current-user-owned, single-link (`st_nlink == 1`) regular file with
mode `0644`; `lstat`, canonical bytes, and digest recomputation are all required
before its contents are trusted. A lock symlink, hardlink, extra top-level
entry, wrong owner/mode, or non-canonical bytes is a collision/blocker even when
reading it would currently yield expected bytes.

Release finalization uses a restrictive sibling temporary directory on the
same filesystem, verifies exact closure, seals file/directory modes, and uses a
platform-proven atomic no-replace rename into the content-addressed destination;
an adapter without that primitive is `BLOCKED`. If that digest destination
already exists, the operation is verify-only. An exact match is reused; any
missing, extra, linked, mode-changed, or content-changed entry is
`BLOCKED_RELEASE_COLLISION` and is never overwritten. Discovery switching uses
a sibling temporary symlink plus atomic no-replace when the approved pre-state
is absent, or atomic exchange when it is present. The displaced entry is
retained and verified before it can become compensation material; unconditional
`os.replace` is forbidden. The same verifier runs before plan acceptance,
promotion, fresh discovery, rollback, and cleanup.

### 8. Transaction, receipt, and rollback

Except for the fixed Bootstrap-root initializer defined in Section 6, every
public or internal entrypoint first acquires the one controller operation lock
and holds it through journal detection, bound-state reads, external probes,
report/plan/receipt writes, verification, and any transaction. The normal lock
is a current-user-owned mode-`0600`, single-link regular file opened no-follow
under the validated state root; bootstrap uses the lock embedded in the stable
Bootstrap Control Root and selects one manifest workspace only after acquiring
it. The held descriptor's device/inode is revalidated and the lock path is never
replaced. An unsafe/unavailable lock blocks rather than running concurrently.
This serialization prevents an audit/scheduled job from observing a mixed
registry or target state during apply/registry/schedule mutation. The separate
short authority lock follows the fixed nesting/linearization rule in Section 6
and does not weaken operation serialization.

Every Router-owned mutation—apply, `bootstrap-apply`, post-success rollback,
`registry-replace`, schedule install/remove/replace, or retained-material
cleanup—rechecks its plan and current action-bound approval while holding that
lock and writes a canonical transaction journal before its first target
mutation. Normal journals live under the validated controller state root; the
first bootstrap journal and all of its authority/evidence live only under the
selected immutable Bootstrap authorization workspace until the bound state root
is installed. For apply, it also creates restrictive temporary compensation
material outside every discovery root, validates the candidate, and finalizes
an immutable release.

While the original approval is still current and before the first
`MUTATION_INTENT`, the Router prepares and fsyncs a canonical single-use
compensation lease. The lease binds the transaction/plan/approval hashes, exact
pre-state, restoration-only path set, compensation hashes, and terminal
conditions. It becomes active only when the fsynced `MUTATION_INTENT`
transition rechecks the still-current, unrevoked approval and binds the lease
hash while holding the authority lock through the first mutation and durable
boundary result. Every forward mutation and terminal-success boundary repeats
that linearized check. Expiry or revocation before intent blocks without target
mutation or active lease; after intent it stops the next forward boundary and
permits only lease-bound restoration/verification.
The lease never authorizes a new candidate, forward resume, post-success
rollback, registry/schedule change, or retained cleanup. A durable success
receipt or verified restoration consumes it. Missing, corrupt, or ambiguous
lease evidence is `RECOVERY_REQUIRED`, never guessed authority.

The journal binds the lease hash, pre-state fingerprint, allowed-path set,
compensation material, and phase. The controller atomically rewrites and
`fsync`s the journal and its containing directory before and after each mutation
boundary. Compensation bytes, their hashes, and containing directories are
durable before the journal authorizes mutation. Each filesystem rename/switch
also fsyncs the affected parent directory before advancing phase.

Every final-child mutation uses a platform-proven conditional protocol anchored
to the verified parent. Creation of an expected-absent child uses atomic
no-replace. Replacement of an expected-present child uses atomic exchange (on
macOS, `renameatx_np(..., RENAME_SWAP)`) with a sibling candidate so the
displaced object is preserved. Removal first exchanges the expected child with
a unique journal-bound tombstone, verifies the displaced object, then uses
atomic no-replace to relocate that exact tombstone into an absent private
quarantine slot and verifies its identity, leaving the destination absent. The
displaced object's type, device/inode, mode, link metadata/target, and content
hash must match the approved pre-state at every step. A mismatch is exchanged
or moved back when unambiguous; no mismatched object is unlinked, and concurrent
ambiguity becomes `RECOVERY_REQUIRED`. The approved removed object remains
preserved as compensation until a durable receipt/restoration permits its
transaction-bound cleanup. Terminal unlink is allowed only inside a
transaction-exclusive current-user-owned private closure whose directory
descriptor, owner/mode, path identity, entry set, and entry identities remain
bound through deletion; if an adapter cannot prove that envelope or observes
drift, it retains the quarantine and returns a cleanup blocker. Unconditional
`os.replace`, check-then-overwrite, check-then-unlink, and adapters without
every required primitive are blocked. Apply then conditionally switches the
discovery symlink and performs:

- target validator/tests;
- planned hash/ref parity;
- effective runtime version observation;
- fresh Skill discovery in a new process/session where practical;
- dependency-group audit.

Every mutation runs its plan-specific verification, then writes and fsyncs its
canonical receipt and marks the journal complete. Every entrypoint detects an
incomplete journal before audit, plan, verify, apply, rollback, schedule, or
cleanup. Public and scheduled paths return `RECOVERY_REQUIRED` and re-enter the
Router without target mutation. The Router-owned internal recovery acquires the
same lock and uses the compensation lease rather than treating an expired
approval as forward authority. Each target-changing substep first durably
records its pre-state and `MUTATION_INTENT`; only a `PREPARED` phase with exact
proof that the target still equals pre-state may remove journal-bound staging
without restoration. Recovery from `MUTATION_INTENT` or any later incomplete
phase treats the target as possibly changed, compares current/pre-state and
expected post-state evidence, and restores and verifies the journal-bound prior
state unless a durable success receipt already proves completion. Phase alone
never proves that a mutation did not occur. A hard kill, process restart, or
machine reboot therefore cannot silently accept an unverified candidate.
Missing, corrupt, or ambiguous journal/compensation evidence returns
`RECOVERY_REQUIRED`/`BLOCKED_RECOVERY_REQUIRED`, records owner and resume
condition, and permits no further mutation. A verified failed transaction
returns `FAILED_COMPENSATED`, not a generic success or receipt.

Receipt fields include plan/approval/compensation-lease identity and hashes,
action, before/after fingerprints, validation results, discovery/version evidence, rollback
availability/hash, timestamps, and sanitized status. They never include
credentials, environment values, private prompts, raw external traces, or file
contents.

After success, the current and immediately previous verified immutable releases
and their receipts are retained outside discovery roots. A user-requested
post-success rollback is a new receipt-bound mutation and requires its own
approval artifact. Before switching, rollback recomputes and matches an
adapter-specific, receipt-bound canonical after-fingerprint. Common fields are
discovery-entry `lstat` metadata and `readlink` value, resolved target,
registry revision/hash, and allowed-path-set hash. `source-runtime` and
`installer-snapshot` also bind the exact payload lock/content/modes.
`git-symlink` also binds repository identity and Git-dir/worktree real paths,
object format, HEAD object and tree, symbolic ref or detached state, index
checksum, remote/ref mapping, submodule state, canonical porcelain status, and
a path/type/mode/content digest inventory of every tracked and untracked
worktree entry outside Git internals. Any dirty, untracked, HEAD/tree/ref,
index, submodule, metadata, or content drift therefore blocks rollback. Other
adapters must define equally complete after-state fields before rollback can be
eligible. Drift in any component or later user change blocks automatic rollback
rather than being overwritten. Cleanup is also separately approved, cannot
remove the current or only known-good prior release, and records the material
as removed. If receipt-bound material is missing, corrupt, or already cleaned,
rollback is `BLOCKED` rather than reconstructed from an unverified source.

Transaction-local staging and compensation are removed under the original
action only while its approval remains current and only after a durable success
receipt or durable verified-restoration record makes them unnecessary. If
expiry/revocation forced lease-only recovery, all leftover local material is
retained inert and requires a separate cleanup plan/approval. Retained
post-success, receipt-bound, rollback, or immutable-release material likewise
requires a separate canonical cleanup plan and `cleanup` approval bound to the
exact receipt, paths, hashes, and expiry.
Journal finalization is not authority to delete retained material. Cleanup also
cannot remove a payload referenced by an installed schedule-execution binding;
that schedule must first be separately removed/replaced and verified.

### 9. Version 1 blocks self-apply

The controller may audit and plan its own source/runtime parity, but version 1
has no self-update transaction adapter. Every apply, post-success rollback, or
cleanup whose resolved target/allowed root aliases the running controller
executable, payload, discovery entry, or release returns
`BLOCKED_SELF_UPDATE`, regardless of package ID. The Router owns the resume
condition: run a separately approved fixed-source deployment outside the live
updater process, then verify discovery in a new process. No nonexistent
bootstrap or automatic self-promotion is claimed.

The first controller installation is the distinct action `bootstrap-apply`, not
self-apply. After source validation and High Review PASS, the Router runs from a
fixed executor whose resolved code/payload/source identities are disjoint from
every target. It writes the fresh immutable plan only to the current
manifest-hash workspace in the Bootstrap Control Root.
`bootstrap-apply` is eligible only when fresh process/discovery/registry
evidence proves there is no running or active discovered updater installation;
exact inactive partial pre-state may be bound for recovery, but an active or
ambiguous installation cannot be updated through the bootstrap action.
The plan binds the external Router executable/code/source identity and hashes;
absent/existing pre-state; updater source/runtime-lock/candidate hashes;
the exact canonical initial registry bytes/schema/revision/hash; complete
package/entry/projection closure and adapter identities/schemas; the full
initial controller-state layout; allowed paths, backup, validation, rollback,
Major authorization, and expiry. A separate exact `bootstrap-apply` approval is
recorded in that workspace before any destination backup or mutation.

After approval, the external Router builds and verifies the complete initial
state tree in a bound same-filesystem sibling, installs the state root with the
conditional final-child protocol, imports the exact plan/approval/lease and
later sanitized receipt by hash, and keeps its transaction journal in the
selected Bootstrap authorization workspace through verified completion.
Package ID equality alone
does not make this external transaction self-apply; executor-to-target
filesystem aliasing does. A live updater process, or any executor aliasing the
target payload/discovery/release, remains blocked. Approval of this Major
implementation contract alone does not authorize the bootstrap transaction.

### 10. Security and Git boundaries

- Registry paths are resolved, contained, and checked for symlink escapes.
- Observation and notification use closed built-in adapters with fixed absolute
  executables/argv; registry and remote text never select code.
- Validation uses JSON argv arrays without a shell and runs only in declared
  candidate roots.
- Remote/ref text is data, never interpolated into shell source.
- Reports redact sensitive categories and do not echo environment values.
- Backup and state roots use restrictive permissions.
- The global ownership graph makes every package's state, OS-temporary
  candidate, compensation, backup, discovery, authoritative source, runtime,
  release, projection, and managed target roots mutually disjoint across
  packages. It rejects containment and filesystem aliases, not only equal path
  strings. State writes fail closed on ownership, permission, symlink, or root
  identity ambiguity.
- Manager/OS-owned state, temporary, discovery, and release containers are
  non-package graph anchors. Only reviewed fixed-layout child entries/subtrees
  may be leased to controller roles, transactions, or packages; a
  whole-container package claim, cross-domain overlapping lease, or operation
  addressed to an anchor rather than the bound child is blocked.
- Filesystem operations use no-follow, directory-FD-anchored traversal or a
  platform-proven equivalent and bind existing components by device/inode. A
  not-yet-existing child is anchored to its verified parent. The controller
  rechecks bound identities immediately before and after mutation, uses atomic
  no-replace for creation, atomic exchange preserving the displaced object for
  replacement, and the exchange/tombstone/exclusive-relocation protocol for
  removal. A component/symlink swap therefore restores or preserves the
  drifted object and blocks instead of consuming a stale resolved string. No
  identity-mismatched object is unlinked; an adapter without an equivalent safe
  primitive is blocked.
- Two projection relationships are intentionally allowed only when declared by
  the registry and verified with `lstat`/`readlink`: a discovery symlink may
  point to its exact immutable release payload, and a collection projection may
  point to its exact authoritative package root. Their lexical entry paths stay
  distinct while their resolved targets must equal the declared contained
  target; chained, escaping, unexpected, or multiply sourced projections are
  rejected.
- Audit and execution of an installed schedule never perform target mutation;
  schedule install/remove/replace follows its own governed transaction.
- A plan request may materialize only its fixed ephemeral Git candidate, and an
  exact apply approval may create only its allowed immutable Git checkout.
  Neither may initialize or mutate sibling/source/live repositories.
- No plan, receipt, Major contract approval, or managed-Skill action in this
  workflow grants staging, commit, branch/tag/remote mutation, reset, clean,
  push, PR, release, plugin installation, or publication authority. The later
  exact `bootstrap-apply` approval grants only its bound updater deployment
  paths and no repository/publication operation. The sole exception is the
  separately explicit exact source-implementation approval for task 2.1, which
  may create only the disclosed local `add-codex-skill-update` branch ref and
  isolated worktree; it grants no staging, commit, remote, push, PR, release,
  or publication authority.

#### Task 2.1 source-worktree bootstrap

That exception is bound by `source_bootstrap_prestate_sha256`. The digest is
SHA-256 over the exact UTF-8 prefix
`source-worktree-bootstrap-prestate-v1` plus one NUL and canonical sorted-key
compact JSON containing exactly `schema` (integer `1`), `repository`,
`git_executable`, `base_commit`, `base_tree`, `symbolic_head`, `refs`,
`worktrees`, `local_config_sha256`, `parent_closure`, and `target_absence`.
`repository` contains exactly `repository_root`, `repository_root_dev`,
`repository_root_ino`, `git_common_dir`, `git_common_dir_dev`,
`git_common_dir_ino`, and `object_format`. `git_executable` contains exactly
`path`, `sha256`, and `version`. Each `refs` entry contains exactly `name`,
`object_oid`, and `object_type`, sorted by the UTF-8 bytes of those fields.
Each `worktrees` entry contains exactly `path`, `head`, and `branch`, where
`branch` is the full ref or JSON `null` when detached, sorted by path bytes.
`local_config_sha256` hashes the exact no-follow, single-link common
`.git/config` bytes with UTF-8 prefix `source-worktree-local-config-v1` plus one
NUL; the config is strictly parsed before Git runs.

`parent_closure` is path-bytewise sorted. Every entry contains exactly `path`,
`type` (`directory`), `dev`, `ino`, `uid`, `mode`, and `nlink`. It includes
every existing component from the filesystem anchors through the repository
root, common Git directory, `.git/refs/heads`, `.git/logs/refs/heads`,
`.git/worktrees`, and the disclosed worktree parent. No component may be a
symlink or be group/world writable at a mutable manager-owned anchor.
`target_absence` contains exactly `branch_ref`, `branch_reflog_path`,
`worktree_path`, and `worktree_admin_path`, each with value `ABSENT`.
Paths, hashes, versions, refs, OIDs, types, and absence values are JSON strings;
`dev`, `ino`, `uid`, and `nlink` are integers; `mode` is a four-digit lowercase
octal string. No undeclared key or JSON coercion is accepted.

The immutable Major manifest also binds that prestate digest, the exact base
commit/tree, Git executable identity, and a path-to-SHA-256 map for every
approved untracked source/supporting file copied into the worktree. Any
repository/Git-dir identity drift, wrong base, changed ref/worktree/config
inventory, or present target component requires reapproval; no existing branch,
worktree, admin directory, or partial residue is reused, repaired, deleted, or
adopted.

Before mutation, the executor creates and fsyncs a canonical source-bootstrap
journal under the approval directory, opens every parent-closure component
no-follow, retains all descriptors, and acquires the current-user-owned
single-link mode-`0600` exclusive `approvals/source-bootstrap.lock`. Under the
held worktree-parent descriptor it reserves the exact target directory with
atomic `mkdirat` no-replace, mode `0700`, records its device/inode in the
journal, and retains its descriptor. The branch ref, reflog, and exact admin
child remain absent for Git's own no-replace lock/create operations.

The executor launches the manifest-bound `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec`, the
manifest-bound descriptor helper, and Git binaries with a manifest-bound canonical
sandbox profile. The profile denies network and every process execution except
the exact helper and manifest-bound Git binary, and denies all filesystem writes
except the exact
branch-ref/ref-lock, branch-reflog/log-lock, reserved target directory, exact
worktree-admin child. The helper is fixed by manifest identity and MUST bind the
retained repository and target descriptors through a manifested FD protocol:
`GIT_DIR=/dev/fd/<repo-fd>` and `fchdir(target-fd)`; it then runs fixed Git
argv `worktree add --no-checkout -b add-codex-skill-update . <base-commit>`.
After acquiring those descriptors and the retained mutable-parent directory
descriptors for `.git/refs/heads`, `.git/logs/refs/heads`, and `.git/worktrees`,
helper SHALL re-`fstat` every retained descriptor and prove they still match the
prestate identities immediately before first Git open; any mismatch blocks before
`exec`ing Git.
Before first Git open, helper SHALL consume those mutable-parent descriptors via a
platform-proven descriptor-rooted operation path (for example `openat`/`openat2`-style
calls and no-Rename parent write gating). If unavailable, task 2.1 SHALL block
before launching Git.
No mutable pathname argument for repository or target is passed through Git parsing.
It forbids `--force`, `-B`, checkout, switch, reset, clean, fetch, clone,
remote, submodule, maintenance, credential, and arbitrary Git subcommands.
`--no-checkout` prevents checkout filters and `post-checkout`.
System/global config and inherited `GIT_CONFIG_*` injection are disabled;
`HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` point to restrictive empty directories; hooks,
fsmonitor, untracked cache, recursion, pagers, prompts, askpass, credential
helpers, protocol helpers, automatic maintenance, and network access are
disabled. Before the first Git process, the descriptor-read local config
rejects keys capable of naming executable hooks, filters, diff/merge drivers,
helpers, aliases, includes, protocol rewrites, submodule commands, worktree
config, or maintenance.

Immediately before sandbox launch and after Git exits, every retained
descriptor is revalidated against `parent_closure`, every lexical component is
re-opened no-follow and compared to the retained identity, and the reserved
target path must still name the retained inode. The sandbox profile itself is
stored no-overwrite at
`approvals/source-bootstrap/profiles/<profile-sha256>.sb`. Its
`write_allowlist_sha256` hashes the exact UTF-8 prefix
`source-worktree-write-allowlist-v1`, one NUL, and canonical sorted-key compact
